d he said well  i'll sign up for it too i want to learn  how to use computers as well it'd be  really handy  so it started in  the spring semester so it meant it  started in the middle of winter  and i don't remember how many sessions  there were maybe it was eight or so  but every saturday for a while we would  you know make the drive in to school and  he and i  would you know park the car and walk to  the the main building  and go in there and there'd be somebody  and i don't know if they actually  worked for uls or if they were somebody  else coming in from the outside  but we would get these these classes and  you know sit down and  tippy type the things into the computer  some of us were very slow typists  my dad actually was a pretty quick  typist and you know we learned  um the basics of what computers were  about and you know  what were the different components and  what could you do and what couldn't you  do  and they there was a book that we  checked out from the library i remember  and it had all these these programs in  it these dos programs  that had you know all these lines and  then it would tell you what the program  did  and i i remember reading through it and  getting kind of excited about it and  like he  my dad and i are looking through it and  being like well that that's kind of a  cool program  maybe we'll have to see if we can  program that in  next time that we get a chance it never  actually  happened like that except for with some  of some of the very short programs  because some of the programs would be  page after page after page  this was a time too by the way when  there were magazines that you could buy  that had all sorts of you know long dos  programs in them as well as  you know all sorts of other computer  related things  that were quite cool so it was like this  this new world that was opening up  and you know i was like uh you know uh  so  1981 i was 10 years old going to be  11 in uh in august  and my dad would have been about 34.  30 35 his birthday was in january  and so it was it was something that we  were doing together and i'll mention  this too as well that  my dad was a very hard worker he  actually was a tax attorney  he had earned a law degree and passed  his cpa  and you know he did a lot of stuff  here in downtown milwaukee actually  we're just a few blocks from where he  used to work  and then he changed jobs so he could be  closer to us  and not be totally gone during tax  season  working as the chief accountant for a  place that  turned out they wanted him to cook the  books whole different story so he ended  up having to leave there but  you know when he had time when he was  when he was you know home on the  weekends or at night and  you know all that he really was engaged  i mean we did all sorts of camping  and we would go we had a boat we'd go  fishing  um you know he took an interest in in  what we were doing  as as did my mom so doing this class  together was not something really that  unusual but it's something that i really  do remember from from our time together  and unfortunately as i think many of you  know my dad died in 1982  um when i was 11 shortly before my  birthday  august 2nd and he was only 36 at the  time  um we were actually supposed to be going  down to  houston texas um he had started working  for  tuschross again and they they wanted him  to open up an office down there so we  were all ready to move  so this is the this would be like the  winter before uh  that year when when he died and and i i  don't think he had you know  any inkling that that was going to to  happen and we certainly  didn't either so this is like something  that that he and i were  doing together to prepare for you know a  future that would include a lot of  computers  and i learned a lot in that class i'm  not sure what he learned because i don't  remember  talking that much about it i think he he  just wanted to actually like  do it with me is something that we would  have fun with and then maybe he'd apply  it  later on and you know i learned the  rudiments  of dos programming i learned a lot of  commands  and how how to understand you know how  how these things  would line up and so it became part of  my repertoire  and you know while i was at uls we would  swap programs here and there people had  written programs  and you know you you would record it on  the first it would you load it up to the  computer then you'd load it on to your  cassette tape that you'd carry around  which you used for all of your your data  and it was all just games  kind of stuff there there was one  program in there that i thought was  really quite cool  which was called eliza and it it was  this is in that book that i was talking  about  it was supposed to replicate a  psychotherapist and it was  you know the very beginning  of a certain kind of a.i you know not a  real ai of course of course but  you see eliza showing up in cyberpunk  novels later on in the 90s like william  gibson  um and other thinkers and i always  wanted to like try to program that in  but it was it was very long  and i didn't have the time to do that so  you know we would putter around with the  computers  they got better unfortunately my dad  like i said died before we could really  reap  many of the benefits of that and but i  but i carried it forward you know  i remember like going over to people's  houses  and you know they would show off the  computer that they had  because that became like a prestige  thing  to have and there were like all sorts of  levels to it you know  if you had a commodore 64 that was  you know kind of a blue blue collar  working class to it's a middle class  kind of thing  the people who had the you know the  really expensive would cost you over a  thousand bucks computers that was you  know the rich people then you had  you know the uh tandy computers in the  middle you had like the zx80 at the very  bottom level  and uh everything in between and you  know dos  wasn't exactly the same in every single  system  but it was it was similar enough that  you'd like go over to somebody's house  and then you start  monkeying around with their computer and  they were usually pretty cool with it  they'd be like wow that kid actually  knows how to program  you know my kids that we bought this for  them and they're not interested in it  all and i  i'd like i was really into adventure  games  and i wanted to create something like  like zorg  you know and so i'd you know we'd be at  somebody's house and i'd be working away  at it  and then of course it was all for naught  because you know  any complex game your your program  you're not going to have the time to to  fully develop it you're not saving it  either so  and then you know by the time that i was  in in high school and we did get a  commodore 64  um we mostly used it for playing games a  bit of word  processing um but i you know i i went  to school um in  in college and my sophomore year  um i got a computer that my friend put  together for me and it was still all  everything was all dos you know um but  things were starting to come out that  were  a lot more complex and so that class  that i took with my dad  um that he wanted to sign up for i think  not  you know maybe because he wanted to do  something with computers but more he  just wanted to hang out with his kid  turned out to be um very useful um  it's not that i do anything with dos  today no nobody really does right but  it proved to be something very helpful  for me  for over a decade and  it was a really nice thing that he did i  remember  you know i have memories of like walking  down the halls with him  and driving out there and driving back  and sitting  by our fireplace in the living room and  like pouring through  this book of programs and talking about  how cool it would be  if we were to program this in so  you know when i sometimes when i see  ancient computers like that ancient  meaning from the 1980s  um those sorts of things come to mind  for me it's  i mean we live in a vastly different  world now where  those computers are basically toys right  not even toys um but that's that's where  we were at that time and for me it's  always connected with this  familial memory and the chance to  reminisce about my my  dad who now has been gone you know  um more than  three quarters of my life  it's a valuable opportunity for me so i  thought i would share that story with  all of you  you

